  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A Noreaster will continue to impact at least \nDowneast Maine into Tuesday AM bringing breezy winds and \nperiods of rain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThe center of low associated with this Noreaster continues to \ndrift NW this afternoon now located along the southern New \nJersey coast. A large occlusion is wrapped up just south of Cape\nCod with a triple point near Georges Bank. Satellite imagery is\nshowing moisture advecting north in Maine from the the Atlantic\nwhich extends back to the Bahamas. This has increased PWATs \nover Downeast Maine into the 175-200 percent of normal range for\nlate September. At the same time a strong 1028mb high pressure \nsystem sits over Central Quebec with a 584dam 500mb ridge. The \nplacement of the ridge and North Atlantic low pressure near \nGreenland is preventing this Noreaster from moving and nearly \ncutoff from the flow. \n\nThroughout this afternoon expect the Noreaster low pressure to \ndrift closer to NYC as surface high drifts into the Maritimes. \nThis will result in lessening precip chances across the CWA \nthrough this afternoon. Tonight, the 500mb ridge will fold over\nand shift out over the North Atlantic which will allow the \nupper level low of this Noreaster to lift back into the mid \nlatitude westerlies. This will begin to push the surface low \nnorthward into Southern New England as it slowly begins to \nweaken. The northerly movement will allow for increasing POPs \nacross the entire CWA late tonight into Monday and lasting into\nTuesday with periods of rain expected. Overall, the wind field \nwill weaken as the sfc low pressure lifts N then NE across the \nGulf of Maine. Then by Tuesday afternoon the system will be \nexiting east across Nova Scotia. Here is what to expect for the \nvarious components of the storm:\n\nPeriods of Rain...Lingering rain chances Downeast and across the\nBangor region this afternoon and early evening, then areawide \nincreasing POPs tonight lasting into Tuesday AM. Although there \nremains significant uncertainty how many periods of rain\ndevelop, the overall trend for slightly lower values has \npersisted into this afternoon. However, with PWATs areawide \neventually up to around 190 percent of normal we can expect some\nbeneficial rainfall. The best beneficial rainfall will be \nacross the Bangor Region to far Southern Aroostook County and \npoints southward to the Downeast coast. It is here that 0.6-1 \ninch is expected for totals, north of here significant \nuncertainty exists due to dry air, totals in northern areas \ngenerally 0.1-0.3 inch. We do not expect any flooding concerns \nanywhere given the D1-D3 drought conditions. In fact nearly all \nof the rainfall will likely be absorbed into the near surface \nground and very unlikely any water gets into the rivers and \nstreams. Rivers and streams are currently in the 1-15th \npercentile range with many approaching top 5 lowest flows on \nrecord. \n\nBreezy Winds...Winds from the northeast will be the gustiest \nthrough Monday AM and begin to weaken into the afternoon and\nMonday night. Winds will be stronger the further south you go \nbut will top out around 25 mph across Bangor and the Downeast \nwith the system remaining so far south of the CWA. Isolated \nhigher gusts possible especially near the Downeast coastline\nexposed to the NE winds across the open waters. We don't \nanticipate impacts from the wind other than maybe a very few\nisolated power outages Downeast.",
